Supreme People's Court

Opinions of the Supreme People's Court on Perfecting the Judicial Accountability System of People's Courts

Fa Fa [2015] No. 13

In order to implement the central authorities' overall plan for deepening judicial system reform, optimize the allocation of trial resources, clarify the authority of adjudication organizations, perfect the judicial accountability system of people's courts, establish and improve a trial power operation mechanism that conforms to the laws of justice, enhance judges' direct involvement in case trials, and ensure that judges independently and impartially perform their adjudication duties in accordance with the law, these Opinions are formulated in accordance with relevant laws and the actual work of people's courts.

1. Goals and Principles

1. Perfecting the judicial accountability system of people's courts must take the strict trial accountability system as the core, the scientific operation mechanism of trial power as the prerequisite, the clear division of authority of trial organizations and duties of trial personnel as the foundation, and the effective trial management and supervision system as the guarantee, so that those who try cases make judgments and those who make judgments bear responsibility, ensuring that people's courts independently and impartially exercise their adjudicative power in accordance with the law.

2. In promoting the reform of the trial accountability system, people's courts shall adhere to the following basic principles:

(1) Uphold the leadership of the Party and adhere to the path of socialist rule of law with Chinese characteristics;

(2) Independently exercise adjudicative power in accordance with the Constitution and laws;

(3) Follow the laws governing the operation of judicial power, reflect the attributes of adjudicative power as the power of judgment and decision, and highlight the principal role of judges in handling cases;

(4) Take adjudicative power as the core, and take the power of trial supervision and trial management as the guarantee;

(5) Have clear powers and responsibilities, unify powers and responsibilities, ensure orderly supervision and effective checks;

(6) Combine subjective fault with objective conduct, and combine responsibility with guarantee.

3. Judges performing their adjudication duties in accordance with the law are protected by law. Judges have the right to independently express opinions on the determination of facts and the application of law. Unless for statutory reasons and through statutory procedures, judges' actions in performing their duties according to law shall not be subject to investigation.

2. Reforming the Operation Mechanism of Adjudicative Power

(1) Operation Mechanism of Single-Judge Panels and Collegial Panels

4. Basic and intermediate people's courts may form trial teams consisting of one judge, judge assistants, court clerks, and other necessary support personnel to independently hear cases subject to summary procedures and other cases as provided by law.

People's courts may, according to the categories of cases accepted, form collegial panels composed of judges or judges and people's jurors through random selection, to hear cases subject to ordinary procedures and cases subject to summary procedures that are required by law to be heard by collegial panels. Basic people's courts with a large number of cases may form relatively fixed trial teams and implement a flat management model.

People's courts shall, based on their functional positioning and trial level, reasonably allocate a certain number of judge assistants, court clerks, and other trial support personnel to judges.

5. On the basis of strengthening specialized trial construction, a case distribution system shall be implemented with random distribution as the main method and designated distribution as the supplementary method. The judge responsible for a case shall be determined randomly according to the category of trial field. If adjustments to the results of random distribution are needed due to special circumstances, the reasons and results of the adjustment shall be made public on the court's work platform.

6. Adjudicative documents formed from cases tried by a single judge shall be signed directly by that judge. Adjudicative documents formed from cases tried by a collegial panel shall be signed successively by the presiding judge, the responsible judge, and other members of the collegial panel; if the presiding judge is also the responsible judge, the presiding judge shall sign last. After the judges of the trial organization have signed successively, the adjudicative document may be printed and issued. Except for cases decided by the adjudicative committee, the president, vice presidents, and division chiefs shall no longer review and sign adjudicative documents for cases in which they did not directly participate in the trial.

The rules for deliberation and voting of collegial panels shall apply the Organic Law of People's Courts, procedural laws, and the Several Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on the Work of Collegial Panels of People's Courts and the Several Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on Further Strengthening the Duties of Collegial Panels.

7. Presidents, vice presidents, full-time members of the adjudicative committee, division chiefs, and deputy division chiefs who are within the judge quota shall handle cases. The annual number of cases handled by presidents, vice presidents, and full-time members of the adjudicative committee shall be reasonably determined by reference to the average number of cases handled by judges of the entire court and based on the amount of trial management and supervision affairs and administrative tasks they undertake. The annual number of cases handled by division chiefs shall be determined by reference to the average number of cases handled by judges of that division. For major, difficult, and complex cases, a collegial panel may be directly formed by the president, vice presidents, and members of the adjudicative committee for trial.

In accordance with the principle of separating adjudicative power from administrative management power, pilot courts may explore the implementation of a centralized management system for administrative affairs such as personnel, finance, and general affairs. If necessary, a vice president may be designated to specifically assist the president in managing administrative affairs.

8. People's courts may respectively establish professional judges' meetings composed of judges from civil, criminal, administrative, and other trial fields to provide advisory opinions for collegial panels on correctly understanding and applying the law. If a collegial panel considers that the case being tried involves a lack of uniformity in the application of law due to its major, difficult, and complex nature, it may submit the issue of law application to a professional judges' meeting for study and discussion. The discussion opinions of the professional judges' meeting are for the collegial panel's reference during reconsideration, and the decision to adopt them rests with the collegial panel. The discussion record shall be placed in the case file for future reference.

A mechanism for legal research on trial business shall be established to unify adjudicative standards through reference to similar cases and case analysis.

(2) Operation Mechanism of the Adjudicative Committee

9. Clarify the function of the adjudicative committee in unifying the adjudicative standards of the court, and reasonably determine the scope of cases discussed by the adjudicative committee according to law. The adjudicative committee shall only discuss major and complex cases involving national diplomacy, security, and social stability, as well as legal application issues in major, difficult, and complex cases. Strengthen the macro-guidance function of the adjudicative committee in summarizing trial experience and discussing and deciding major matters in trial work.

10. If a collegial panel considers that a case needs to be submitted to the adjudicative committee for discussion and decision, it shall propose and list the legal application issues that require discussion and decision by the adjudicative committee, and summarize the different opinions and reasons.

The conditions and procedures for a collegial panel to submit a case to the adjudicative committee for discussion shall apply the Organic Law of People's Courts, procedural laws, and the Several Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on the Work of Collegial Panels of People's Courts and the Implementation Opinions of the Supreme People's Court on Reforming and Improving the Adjudicative Committee System of People's Courts.

11. If a case needs to be submitted to the adjudicative committee for discussion and decision, members of the adjudicative committee shall review the materials submitted by the collegial panel for discussion in advance, understand the different opinions and reasons of the collegial panel on legal application issues, and, as needed, view audio or video recordings of the trial or consult the case file.

When discussing cases, members of the adjudicative committee shall fully express their opinions. The voting order shall be determined from lower to higher judge rank, with the presiding officer voting last. The deliberations of the adjudicative committee shall be fully recorded, including audio and video recordings, and meeting minutes shall be prepared. The decisions of the adjudicative committee shall be implemented by the collegial panel. All members who participated in the discussion and voting shall sign the meeting minutes of the adjudicative committee.

Establish a performance evaluation and internal disclosure mechanism for members of the adjudicative committee. Establish a system for supervision, response, and disclosure of matters resolved by the adjudicative committee.

(3) Trial Management and Supervision

12. Establish a case quality evaluation system and assessment mechanism that conforms to the laws of justice. Trial management and trial supervision organs shall regularly analyze the operational trend of trial quality, and conduct professional evaluations of case quality through routine spot checks, focused reviews, special reviews, and other methods.

13. People's courts at all levels shall establish a judge performance appraisal committee, and establish a performance evaluation system and performance files for judges. The performance files shall mainly include the judge's personal daily performance, number of cases handled, trial quality, judicial skills, integrity and self-discipline, and external evaluations. The performance evaluation of judges shall serve as an important basis for judge appointment, selection for honors, and promotion.

14. People's courts at all levels shall rely on information technology to construct an open, dynamic, transparent, and convenient sunshine judicial mechanism, establish and improve the three major platforms of trial process disclosure, adjudicative document disclosure, and enforcement information disclosure, and widely accept social supervision. Explore the establishment of third-party evaluation mechanisms outside the court to strengthen legal supervision, social supervision, and public opinion supervision over the operation mechanism of adjudicative power.

3. Clarifying the Duties and Authority of Judicial Personnel

(1) Duties of Judicial Personnel in Single-Judge Panels and Collegial Panels

15. When a judge hears a case independently, the judge shall perform the following adjudication duties:

(1) Preside over or guide judge assistants in preparing for trial, including pretrial conferences, pretrial mediation, exchange of evidence, and other trial support work;

(2) Preside over the trial and mediation of the case, render a judgment in accordance with the law, and prepare the adjudicative document or guide the judge assistant in drafting the adjudicative document, and directly sign and issue the adjudicative document;

(3) Decide procedural matters during the trial in accordance with the law;

(4) Exercise other adjudicative powers in accordance with the law.

16. When a collegial panel hears a case, the responsible judge shall perform the following adjudication duties:

(1) Preside over or guide judge assistants in preparing for trial, including pretrial conferences, pretrial mediation, exchange of evidence, and other trial support work;

(2) Submit motions raised by the parties, such as objections to jurisdiction, requests for preservation, judicial authentication, and exclusion of illegally obtained evidence, to the collegial panel for deliberation;

(3) Conduct a comprehensive review of the evidence submitted by the parties and present review opinions;

(4) Draft a trial outline and prepare case review notes;

(5) When serving as the presiding judge, preside over and direct the trial activities; when not serving as the presiding judge, assist the presiding judge in conducting trial activities;

(6) Participate in the deliberation of the case and present a handling opinion first;

(7) Prepare the adjudicative document based on the collegial panel's deliberation opinions or guide the judge assistant in drafting the adjudicative document;

(8) Exercise other adjudicative powers in accordance with the law.

17. When a collegial panel hears a case, the other judges of the collegial panel shall diligently perform their adjudication duties, jointly participate in case review, trial, deliberation, and other trial activities, independently express opinions, review and sign the adjudicative document.

18. When a collegial panel hears a case, the presiding judge, in addition to assuming the adjudication duties shared by the collegial panel members, shall also perform the following adjudication duties:

(1) Determine the trial plan, trial outline, coordinate the division of tasks among collegial panel members, and guide other necessary trial preparations;

(2) Preside over and direct the trial activities;

(3) Preside over the collegial panel's deliberation;

(4) In accordance with relevant regulations and procedures, submit cases with significant differences of opinion among collegial panel members to the professional judges' meeting for discussion, or propose to submit the case to the adjudicative committee for discussion and decision according to procedures;

(5) Exercise other adjudicative powers in accordance with the law.

When the presiding judge also handles a case as the responsible judge, the presiding judge shall simultaneously perform the duties of the responsible judge.

19. Judge assistants shall perform the following duties under the guidance of a judge:

(1) Review litigation materials and assist the judge in organizing the pretrial exchange of evidence;

(2) Assist the judge in organizing pretrial mediation and draft mediation documents;

(3) Upon delegation by the judge or assist the judge in handling property preservation and evidence preservation measures in accordance with the law;

(4) Upon assignment by the judge, handle matters such as entrusted authentication and evaluation;

(5) According to the judge's requirements, prepare reference materials related to the trial of the case and study relevant legal issues involved in the case;

(6) Draft adjudicative documents under the guidance of the judge;

(7) Complete other trial support work assigned by the judge.

20. Court clerks shall, under the guidance of a judge and in accordance with relevant regulations, perform the following duties:

(1) Be responsible for the administrative work of pretrial preparation;

(2) Check the appearance of litigation participants at trial and announce court rules;

(3) Be responsible for recording during the trial of the case;

(4) Organize, bind, and archive case file materials;

(5) Complete other administrative work assigned by the judge.

(2) Management and Supervision Duties of Presidents and Division Chiefs

21. In addition to performing relevant adjudication duties in accordance with the law, the president shall also guide the court's various trial work from a macro perspective, organize research on relevant major issues and formulate relevant management systems, be comprehensively responsible for trial management work, preside over the adjudicative committee to discuss major matters in trial work, preside over the judge performance appraisal committee to evaluate judges in accordance with the law, and perform other necessary trial management and supervision duties.

Vice presidents and full-time members of the adjudicative committee, upon delegation by the president, may perform part of the trial management and supervision duties in accordance with the preceding paragraph.

22. In addition to performing relevant adjudication duties in accordance with the law, the division chief shall also guide the trial work of the division from a macro perspective, study and formulate the division of duties among collegial panels and trial teams and among internal members, be responsible for matters requiring case distribution adjustments due to special circumstances after random case distribution, regularly supervise the trial quality of the division, and perform other necessary trial management and supervision duties.

23. The trial management and supervision activities of presidents, vice presidents, and division chiefs shall be strictly limited to the scope of their duties and authority, and shall be conducted openly on the work platform. Except for participating in the adjudicative committee and professional judges' meetings, presidents, vice presidents, and division chiefs shall not express biased opinions on cases in which they did not participate in the trial.

24. For cases with any of the following circumstances, the president, vice presidents, and division chiefs have the right to require the single judge or collegial panel to report the case progress and deliberation results:

(1) Cases involving group disputes that may affect social stability;

(2) Cases that are difficult, complex, and have significant social impact;

(3) Cases that may conflict with similar case decisions of the court or a higher court;

(4) Cases where relevant units or individuals report that a judge has engaged in illegal adjudication.

If the president, vice presidents, or division chiefs have objections to the trial process or deliberation results of the above cases, they shall not directly change the opinion of the collegial panel, but may decide to submit the case to the professional judges' meeting or the adjudicative committee for discussion. The time, content, and results of the supervision suggestions made by presidents, vice presidents, and division chiefs regarding the above cases shall be fully recorded in the case file and on the office platform.

4. Determination and Investigation of Trial Responsibility

(1) Scope of Trial Responsibility

25. Judges shall bear responsibility for their actions in performing their adjudication duties, and shall be permanently responsible for the quality of case handling within the scope of their duties.

If a judge intentionally violates laws or regulations in trial work, or causes erroneous judgments and serious consequences due to gross negligence, the judge shall bear responsibility for illegal adjudication in accordance with the law.

If a judge violates professional ethics and disciplinary regulations, such as accepting invitations and gifts from parties and related persons, or engaging in improper dealings with lawyers, such disciplinary or illegal actions shall be dealt with separately in accordance with the law and relevant disciplinary regulations.

26. Under any of the following circumstances, the responsible persons shall be investigated for illegal adjudication responsibility in accordance with discipline and law:

(1) Engaging in corruption, bribery, favoritism, or perverting the law in adjudication during the trial of a case;

(2) Handling cases privately in violation of regulations or creating false cases;

(3) Altering, concealing, forging, substituting, or intentionally damaging evidence materials, or losing or damaging evidence materials due to gross negligence and causing serious consequences;

(4) Concealing major evidence or important circumstances, or intentionally providing false materials when reporting case facts to the collegial panel or adjudicative committee, or omitting major evidence or important circumstances due to gross negligence, leading to erroneous judgments and causing serious consequences;

(5) Intentionally violating the collegial panel's deliberation results or the adjudicative committee's decision when preparing litigation documents, or causing errors in the main text of the adjudicative document due to gross negligence and causing serious consequences;

(6) Violating legal provisions by granting commutation or parole to a criminal who does not meet the conditions for commutation or parole, or granting commutation or parole to a criminal who does not meet the conditions due to gross negligence and causing serious consequences;

(7) Other intentional violations of statutory procedures, evidence rules, and clear legal provisions in adjudication, or causing erroneous judgment results due to gross negligence and causing serious consequences.

27. If personnel with supervisory and management responsibilities, due to intentional conduct or gross negligence, neglect or improperly exercise trial supervision and trial management powers, leading to erroneous judgments and causing serious consequences, they shall bear supervisory and management responsibility in accordance with relevant regulations. The investigation of such supervisory and management responsibility shall be handled in accordance with relevant regulations and procedures for cadre management.

28. If a case is retried and the judgment is reversed through trial supervision procedures due to any of the following circumstances, it shall not be deemed a wrong case for the purpose of accountability:

(1) There are differences in understanding and interpretation of specific provisions of laws, regulations, rules, or judicial interpretations, which can be reasonably explained within the scope of professional knowledge;

(2) There are disputes or doubts about the determination of basic facts of the case, which can be reasonably explained according to the rules of evidence;

(3) The party waives or partially waives the claim of rights;

(4) The determination of case facts changes due to the party's fault or objective reasons;

(5) The judgment is changed due to the emergence of new evidence;

(6) Laws are revised or policies are adjusted;

(7) Other legal documents on which the judgment is based are revoked or changed;

(8) Other circumstances where responsibility should not be borne due to proper performance of adjudication duties.

(2) Bearing of Trial Responsibility

29. For cases tried under a single-judge system, the single judge shall bear full responsibility for the determination of facts and the application of law.

30. For cases tried by a collegial panel, the members of the collegial panel shall jointly bear responsibility for the determination of facts and the application of law.

When investigating responsibility for illegal adjudication, the respective responsibilities shall be reasonably determined based on whether the collegial panel members engaged in illegal adjudication, the circumstances, the opinions expressed by the collegial panel members, and the degree of fault.

31. When the adjudicative committee discusses a case, the collegial panel is responsible for the facts it reports, and the members of the adjudicative committee are responsible for the opinions they express and their final vote.

If a case is discussed by the adjudicative committee and circumstances constituting investigation of illegal adjudication liability arise, the responsibilities of the committee members shall be reasonably determined based on whether they intentionally distorted the law in expressing opinions. If the adjudicative committee changes the opinion of the collegial panel, leading to an erroneous judgment, the committee members holding the majority opinion shall jointly bear responsibility, and the collegial panel shall not bear responsibility. If the adjudicative committee upholds the opinion of the collegial panel, leading to an erroneous judgment, the collegial panel and the committee members holding the majority opinion shall jointly bear responsibility.

If the collegial panel, when reporting a case, intentionally conceals major evidence or important circumstances, or intentionally provides false information, leading the adjudicative committee to make an erroneous decision, the collegial panel members shall bear responsibility, and the members of the adjudicative committee shall bear partial responsibility or no responsibility depending on the specific circumstances.

If the adjudicative committee's discussion of a case violates the principle of democratic centralism, leading to an erroneous decision by the adjudicative committee, the presiding officer shall bear primary responsibility.

32. Trial support personnel shall bear responsibility corresponding to their duties according to their authority and division of work. If a judge has the duty of review and verification, the judge shall also bear corresponding responsibility.

33. If a judge suffers interference from leading cadres resulting in an erroneous judgment, and the judge fails to record or accurately record the interference, or fails to exclude interference when it should be excluded, the judge shall bear responsibility for illegal adjudication.

(3) Procedures for Investigating Responsibility for Illegal Adjudication

34. Where disciplinary liability for unlawful adjudication needs to be investigated, preliminary opinions shall generally be submitted by the President, the Adjudication Supervision Department, or the Adjudication Management Department. The President shall entrust the Adjudication Supervision Department to review the matter or submit it to the Adjudication Committee for discussion. If, upon review, it is preliminarily determined that any relevant person falls within the circumstances requiring investigation of disciplinary liability for unlawful adjudication as listed in these Opinions, the People's Court's Supervisory Department shall initiate the disciplinary liability investigation procedures.

People's courts at all levels shall consciously accept supervision by the People's Congress, the Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference, the media, and society in accordance with the law, and shall lawfully accept reports and complaints regarding judges' unlawful adjudication conduct, and conduct earnest investigations and verifications.

35. The Supervisory Department of the People's Court shall investigate whether a judge has engaged in unlawful adjudication conduct and shall take necessary and reasonable protective measures. During the investigation, the judge concerned has the right to know, to defend, and to adduce evidence. The Supervisory Department shall truthfully record the opinions, defenses, and evidence submitted by the judge concerned, and shall explain in the investigation report whether such submissions have been adopted.

36. Where the Supervisory Department of the People's Court, after investigation, considers that a judge should be subject to disciplinary liability for unlawful adjudication, it shall report to the President for decision, and shall submit the matter to the Provincial (Autonomous Region, Municipality) Judges' Disciplinary Committee for deliberation.

The Supervisory Department of the Higher People's Court shall send personnel to inform the Judges' Disciplinary Committee of the facts of the judge's unlawful adjudication and the proposed handling recommendations and their basis, and shall adduce evidence regarding the unlawful adjudication conduct and the judge's subjective fault. The judge concerned has the right to make statements, adduce evidence, defend, apply for reconsideration, and appeal.

The Judges' Disciplinary Committee shall, based on the facts found and the law, make recommendations of no liability, exemption from liability, or the imposition of disciplinary sanctions.

The working rules and disciplinary procedures of the Judges' Disciplinary Committee shall be separately formulated.

37. Relevant responsible persons who should be subject to disciplinary liability for unlawful adjudication shall be dealt with in accordance with the relevant provisions of the Judges Law of the People's Republic of China, based on the liability they bear:

(1) Where a sanction such as suspension of duties, postponement of promotion, removal from the judges' quota, or removal from office, compulsory resignation, or dismissal should be imposed, it shall be handled by the organizational and personnel department according to the authority and procedures for cadre management;

(2) Where disciplinary sanctions should be imposed, it shall be handled by the disciplinary inspection and supervision department in accordance with the relevant provisions and procedures;

(3) Where criminal conduct is suspected, the disciplinary inspection and supervision department shall transfer the unlawful leads to the relevant judicial authorities for handling in accordance with the law.

Removal of a judge from office must be carried out by means of impeachment by the People's Congress or a request to the Standing Committee of the People's Congress for a decision, in accordance with statutory procedures.

V. Strengthening Safeguards for Judges' Performance of Duties

38. At all stages of case adjudication, unless there is conclusive evidence that a judge has engaged in serious unlawful adjudication conduct such as bribery, embezzlement, favoritism, or misjudgment, the judge's lawful performance of duties shall not be suspended or terminated.

39. Judges' lawful adjudication shall not be interfered with by administrative organs, social organizations, or individuals. Any organization or individual that illegally interferes with judicial activities, inquires about or meddles in the handling of specific cases, shall be recorded, reported, and held accountable in accordance with the relevant provisions.

Interference by leading cadres in judicial activities, meddling in specific cases, and inquiries into cases by internal judicial personnel shall be handled separately in accordance with the Provisions on the Recording, Reporting, and Accountability for Leading Cadres Interfering in Judicial Activities and Meddling in the Handling of Specific Cases and the Provisions on the Recording and Accountability for Internal Judicial Personnel Inquiring into Cases, as well as their implementation measures.

40. Where a judge's reputation is damaged due to false reports or malicious accusations made against him or her because of the lawful performance of duties, or where it is determined by the Judges' Disciplinary Committee or other organizations that no legal or disciplinary liability should be pursued, the People's Court's Supervisory Department and News and Publicity Department shall promptly clarify the facts within an appropriate scope and in an appropriate form to eliminate adverse effects and maintain the judge's good reputation.

41. Where a People's Court or relevant department makes an erroneous decision regarding a judge, it shall apologize, restore the judge's position and reputation, eliminate the adverse effects, and provide compensation for any economic losses in accordance with the law.

42. Where a judge's grade promotion is temporarily postponed due to acceptance of an investigation, and it is later determined by the relevant department that no disciplinary liability for unlawful adjudication exists, or the Judges' Disciplinary Committee makes a recommendation of no liability or exemption from liability, the promotion time shall be calculated continuously from the date of the postponement.

43. Acts that impede litigation or seriously defy the authority of the court, such as destroying evidence materials, court records, legal documents, or court facilities in court, shall be lawfully and promptly punished. The personal and property safety of judges and their close relatives shall be protected in accordance with the law. Criminal and other illegal acts, such as intimidation, threats, insults, stalking, harassment, or assault against judges and their close relatives, whether inside or outside the courtroom, shall be lawfully and promptly punished.

Where a person infringes upon a judge's personal dignity, or divulges the privacy of a judge or the judge's relatives that cannot be lawfully disclosed, thereby interfering with the judge's lawful performance of duties, the relevant person shall be held accountable in accordance with the law.

44. The severity of punishment for illegal or criminal acts such as obstructing a judge's lawful exercise of adjudicative power, falsely accusing a judge, defying the authority of the court, or seriously disrupting the adjudicative order shall be increased. Supporting systems shall be studied and improved, and relevant laws shall be amended or supplemented.

VI. Supplementary Provisions

45. For the purposes of these Opinions, "judge" means a judge who has entered the judges' quota after selection by the Judges' Selection Committee.

46. The determination and investigation of adjudicative liability under these Opinions apply to judges, deputy presiding judges, presiding judges, full-time members of the Adjudication Committee, vice presidents, and presidents of People's Courts. The determination and investigation of liability for enforcement officers, judge assistants, court clerks, judicial police, and other adjudicative support personnel shall be carried out by reference to these provisions.

The duties of technical investigation officers and other adjudicative support personnel shall be separately provided.

The adjudicative liability in cases involving people's jurors in courts piloting the reform of the people's juror system shall be separately provided in accordance with the Pilot Plan for the Reform of the People's Juror System.

47. The interpretation of these Opinions rests with the Supreme People's Court.

48. These Opinions shall apply to courts piloting the judicial system reform as determined by the Central Committee and to courts piloting the reform of the adjudicative power operation mechanism as determined by the Supreme People's Court.

